</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
  <header>
    <h1>Global Intentional Homicide Trends</h1>
    <p>A Multi-Dimensional Analysis (1990–2023) · UNODC Crime Trends Survey Data</p>
  </header>

  <!-- KPI Dashboard -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Global Median Rate ({meta['most_recent_year']})</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(kpis['global_median_recent'], 2)}</div>
      <div class="kpi-context">per 100,000 population</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent">
      <div class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Ratio ({meta['most_recent_year']})</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(kpis['ratio_median'], 1)}:1</div>
      <div class="kpi-context">Median across {kpis['countries_reporting_recent']} countries</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate ({meta['most_recent_year']})</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(kpis['highest_country']['rate'], 2)}</div>
      <div class="kpi-context">{kpis['highest_country']['country']}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card success">
      <div class="kpi-label">Decade Change (2010s vs 2000s)</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(kpis['decade_changes'][1]['mean_change_pct'], 1)}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-context">Average yearly mean rate</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Reporting Coverage ({meta['most_recent_year']})</div>
      <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(kpis['coverage_recent'], 1)}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-context">{kpis['countries_reporting_recent']} of {meta['total_countries']} countries</div>
    </div>
  </div>
'''

# Section: Global Overview
html += f'''
  <section id="global-overview">
    <h2>1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>
    <p>Over the three decades from 1990 to 2023, the global intentional homicide landscape shows a mixed but broadly improving picture—at least among the countries that report data. The <strong>global median homicide rate</strong> across reporting nations has trended downward from roughly 4.0 per 100,000 in the early 1990s to <strong>{fmt(kpis['global_median_recent'], 2)} per 100,000</strong> in {meta['most_recent_year']}. However, the global mean remains elevated at <strong>{fmt(kpis['global_mean_recent'], 2)}</strong>, driven by a long right tail of high-violence outliers.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,{charts['global_trend']}" alt="Global homicide rate trend">
    </div>

    <p>The divergence between median and mean is instructive: while the typical reporting country has become safer, a subset of nations continues to experience extreme homicide burdens that pull the average upward. This underscores the importance of disaggregating global aggregates before drawing policy conclusions.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,{charts['coverage']}" alt="Data reporting coverage by year">
    </div>

    <div class="disclaimer">
      <strong>Data completeness caveat:</strong> Coverage peaked at roughly 68% of countries in 2019 but fell to {fmt(kpis['coverage_recent'], 1)}% by {meta['most_recent_year']}. The set of reporting countries changes year to year, so global aggregates are not strictly comparable across time. Interpret trends with caution.
    </div>
  </section>
'''

# Section: Regional & Country-Level Comparison
html += f'''
  <section id="regional-comparison">
    <h2>2. Regional & Country-Level Comparison</h2>
    <p>Geographic patterns in homicide are stark. In {meta['most_recent_year']}, <strong>Latin America & the Caribbean</strong> recorded the highest regional mean rate ({fmt(next(r['mean'] for r in data['rankings']['region_recent'] if r['region']=='Latin America & Caribbean'), 1)} per 100,000), followed by North America and South America. By contrast, Europe and Asia reported mean rates below 2.0 per 100,000.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,{charts['regional']}" alt="Regional homicide rates">
    </div>

    <h3>Country Rankings ({meta['most_recent_year']})</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p><strong>Top 10 Highest Rates</strong></p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Rate</th></tr>
'''
for i, r in enumerate(data['rankings']['top10_recent'], 1):
    html += f'          <tr><td>{i}</td><td>{r["country"]}</td><td>{fmt(r["homicide_rate"], 2)}</td></tr>\n'
html += f'''        </table>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>Top 10 Lowest Rates</strong></p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Country</th><th>Rate</th></tr>
'''
for i, r in enumerate(data['rankings']['bottom10_recent'], 1):
    html += f'          <tr><td>{i}</td><td>{r["country"]}</td><td>{fmt(r["homicide_rate"], 2)}</td></tr>\n'
html += f'''        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,{charts['top_bottom']}" alt="Top and bottom countries by homicide rate">
    </div>

    <p>Small island states and territories in the Caribbean dominate the highest-rate list, while East Asian and European microstates consistently report the lowest levels. The concentration of extreme violence in the Americas—both Latin America and parts of the Caribbean—suggests that regional factors (organized crime, firearm availability, state capacity) may be more predictive than national income alone.</p>
  </section>
'''

# Section: Gender Disparity
html += f'''
  <section id="sex-disparity">
    <h2>3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>
    <p>One of the most consistent findings across the dataset is the profound gender imbalance in homicide victimization. In {data['sex_disparity']['latest_year']}, the median male homicide rate across reporting countries was <strong>{fmt(kpis['male_median'], 2)} per 100,000</strong>, compared with <strong>{fmt(kpis['female_median'], 2)} per 100,000</strong> for females—a <strong>{fmt(kpis['ratio_median'], 1)}-to-1</strong> ratio.</p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Males are disproportionately victimized by intentional homicide in virtually every reporting country. The median ratio has remained relatively stable over time, indicating that while absolute rates may rise or fall, the relative burden on men persists.
    </div>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,{charts['sex_ratio_time']}" alt="Male-to-female homicide ratio over time">
    </div>

    <h3>Highest Male-to-Female Ratios ({data['sex_disparity']['latest_year']})</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Country</th><th>Male Rate</th><th>Female Rate</th><th>Ratio</th></tr>
'''
for r in data['sex_disparity']['countries'][:10]:
    html += f'      <tr><td>{r["country"]}</td><td>{fmt(r["male"], 2)}</td><td>{fmt(r["female"], 2)}</td><td>{fmt(r["ratio"], 1)}:1</td></tr>\n'
html += f'''    </table>

    <p>Barbados, Saint Kitts and Nevis, Grenada, and Colombia show the most lopsided ratios, with male victimization rates more than ten times those of females. This pattern is consistent with the literature on lethal violence: males are far more likely to be victims of firearm violence and organized-crime-related homicides, whereas female victimization is more often linked to intimate-partner violence—a distinct policy challenge requiring targeted interventions.</p>
  </section>
'''

# Section: Age Group Analysis
html += f'''
  <section id="age-group-analysis">
    <h2>4. Age Group Analysis</h2>
    <p>Because the dataset does not report age-disaggregated totals by sex combined, we average available male and female age-specific rates where both are present. The resulting pattern is unambiguous: <strong>young adults face the highest homicide risk globally</strong>.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,{charts['age_groups']}" alt="Homicide rates by age group and sex">
    </div>

    <p>The peak risk window is <strong>ages 25–29</strong>, with a global mean rate of {fmt(next(r['mean'] for r in data['age']['combined'] if r['age_group']=='25_29'), 1)} per 100,000, followed closely by ages 20–24 ({fmt(next(r['mean'] for r in data['age']['combined'] if r['age_group']=='20_24'), 1)}). Risk declines steadily with age, though the 30–44 bracket remains elevated relative to adolescents and children.</p>

    <p>For males, the concentration is even more pronounced: the mean rate for males aged 25–29 is {fmt(next(r['mean'] for r in data['age']['male'] if r['age_group']=='25_29'), 1)} per 100,000—roughly ten times the female rate in the same cohort ({fmt(next(r['mean'] for r in data['age']['female'] if r['age_group']=='25_29'), 1)}). Children under 10 and ages 10–14 experience the lowest rates across both sexes, although even these baseline levels are not zero.</p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>Policy implication:</strong> Violence-prevention programs should prioritize young adult males, particularly in high-violence regions. Early intervention in adolescence (ages 15–17), where rates begin to climb sharply, may yield disproportionate returns.
    </div>
  </section>
'''

# Section: Notable Trends
html += f'''
  <section id="notable-trends">
    <h2>5. Notable Country-Level Trends & Outliers</h2>
    <p>Beyond snapshots, the longitudinal dimension reveals dramatic stories of both improvement and deterioration. The following countries experienced the largest absolute changes over observation spans of at least ten years.</p>

    <div class="chart">
      <img src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,{charts['notable_trends']}" alt="Notable country-level trends">
    </div>

    <h3>Most Significant Improvements</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Country</th><th>Period</th><th>Start</th><th>End</th><th>Change</th><th>Slope/Year</th></tr>
'''
for r in data['trends']['top_decreases'][:8]:
    html += f'      <tr><td>{r["country"]}</td><td>{r["first_year"]}–{r["last_year"]}</td><td>{fmt(r["first_rate"], 2)}</td><td>{fmt(r["last_rate"], 2)}</td><td><span class="tag tag-down">{fmt(r["total_change"], 2)}</span></td><td>{fmt(r["slope_per_year"], 3)}</td></tr>\n'
html += f'''    </table>

    <h3>Most Significant Deteriorations</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Country</th><th>Period</th><th>Start</th><th>End</th><th>Change</th><th>Slope/Year</th></tr>
'''
for r in data['trends']['top_increases'][:8]:
    html += f'      <tr><td>{r["country"]}</td><td>{r["first_year"]}–{r["last_year"]}</td><td>{fmt(r["first_rate"], 2)}</td><td>{fmt(r["last_rate"], 2)}</td><td><span class="tag tag-up">+{fmt(r["total_change"], 2)}</span></td><td>{fmt(r["slope_per_year"], 3)}</td></tr>\n'
html += f'''    </table>

    <p><strong>El Salvador</strong> stands out as the most dramatic improvement: from a peak observation of 134.3 per 100,000 in 1994 to 7.9 in 2022—a decline that likely reflects both policy interventions and shifting gang dynamics, though data gaps in intervening years temper certainty. <strong>Colombia</strong> also recorded a remarkable halving of its homicide rate over three decades.</p>

    <p>On the deterioration side, several small Caribbean states—<strong>Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, and Saint Lucia</strong>—have seen rates climb to among the world’s highest. These trends warrant urgent regional attention, given small population sizes and limited institutional capacity.</p>
  </section>
'''

# Section: Data Limitations & Policy Recommendations
html += f'''
  <section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
    <h2>6. Data Limitations & Policy Recommendations</h2>

    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> Not all countries report every year. The composition of the global sample shifts over time, which can introduce bias into cross-year comparisons. A drop in the global mean may reflect the addition of low-violence reporters rather than a true universal decline.</li>
      <li><strong>Coverage decline:</strong> After peaking at ~68% of countries in 2019, coverage fell to {fmt(kpis['coverage_recent'], 1)}% by {meta['most_recent_year']}. This limits confidence in recent global estimates.</li>
      <li><strong>United Kingdom caveat:</strong> UK data cover England and Wales only; Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately. Analysts should avoid treating UK figures as fully national.</li>
      <li><strong>Territories:</strong> Some territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) appear as separate entries. Care must be taken to avoid double-counting with parent states.</li>
      <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing years were not interpolated. Any averaging or trend analysis is conditional on the years actually observed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>1. Target young adult males in high-violence regions.</strong> The 18–29 age group, particularly males, accounts for the steepest risk gradient. Violence-reduction programs, employment initiatives, and hotspot policing should be concentrated here.
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>2. Address the Caribbean crisis.</strong> Multiple small island states have experienced explosive homicide growth. Regional cooperation on firearms trafficking, forensic capacity, and justice reform is urgently needed.
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>3. Learn from success stories.</strong> Colombia and El Salvador demonstrate that sustained declines are possible. Rigorous evaluation of the policies and contextual factors behind these improvements should be prioritized.
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>4. Close the gender lens.</strong> While males bear the bulk of homicide victimization, female homicide—often intimate-partner related—requires distinct prevention frameworks. Sex-disaggregated reporting should be universal, not optional.
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>5. Invest in data infrastructure.</strong> Global monitoring capacity is weakening. Donors and international bodies should support national statistical and criminal-justice information systems, especially in Africa and parts of Asia where coverage remains thin.
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p>Report generated from UNODC Crime Trends Survey data (1990–2023) · Analysis derived directly from <code>data.csv</code> · No external sources or imputed values used.</p>
  </footer>
</div>
</body>
</html>
'''

with open('output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print("Report written to output/final_report.html")
PYEOF

